{"type":"text","timestamp":1794358076364,"sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","part":{"id":"prt_1c81363bd001ywr2ndUj0gPfVz","messageID":"msg_1c81350fc001u8dreS735yirCC","sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","type":"text","text":"<think>Fix: the `priority` state type should be `Priority`. But the API accepts extra priorities; the task's priority is typed as Priority. Let me type the state as `Priority` and cast when reading task.priority. The API type says Priority = \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\", but the server can return \"urgent\"/\"critical\" — those wouldn't match the type. Let me widen the Priority type in types.ts to include the extra values the server accepts, and use the `PRIORITIES` list for the UI options.\n\nLet me update types.ts:\n\n```ts\nexport type Priority = \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\" | \"urgent\" | \"critical\" | \"normal\";\n```\n\nAnd in TaskDialog: `const [priority, setPriority] = useState<Priority>(...)`.\n\nAlso `payload.priority = priority` — the api.updateTask payload type is `Partial<Omit<Task, \"id\"|\"updated_at\">>` → priority: Priority ✓.\n\nAnd api.createTask priority: string ✓.\n\nLet me fix.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794358076350,"end":1794358076362}}}
