{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794358133397,"sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","part":{"id":"prt_1c8144278001CEGnK8m5i1MYyc","messageID":"msg_1c8140c15001t5g76apIBFd2op","sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794358133397,"sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","part":{"id":"prt_1c814427a001IB1suCbtX3W0Ll","messageID":"msg_1c8140c15001t5g76apIBFd2op","sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","type":"text","text":"<think>No general network access — only the package registries (npm/pypi proxy). So no browser download. No browser testing possible.\n\nI'll rely on:\n1. Thorough API testing (done).\n2. Careful frontend code review.\n3. Maybe a JSDOM-based test of some frontend logic? I could install jsdom via npm and test the drag resolution logic in isolation (it's pure-ish logic but depends on DOM APIs like elementFromPoint). jsdom doesn't implement elementFromPoint (it does have document.elementFromPoint? jsdom throws \"not implemented\"). I could mock it.\n\nAlternatively, I can unit-test the pure parts: applyMove, matchesFilter, and the resolveDrop math (by extracting the math into a pure function that takes rects). That would let me verify the position computation with node.\n\nLet me refactor resolveDrop to separate the pure computation from the DOM lookups: a pure function `computeDrop({visible, others, rects, dragged})`. Hmm, the rects come from the DOM. I can write a pure function that takes a list of {task, top, height} plus the pointer Y.\n\nLet me restructure useDragAndDrop: extract a pure helper `computeDropTarget({ taskId, fromStatus, fromPosition, status, cards, pointerY })` where cards = [{id, position, top, height}]. Then I can unit test it with node/vitest? There's no test runner installed. I could write a quick node script using tsx? Not installed. I could compile with esbuild (vite includes esbuild) — I can use `npx esbuild` to bundle a test file and run it with node.\n\nSimpler: write the pure helper in a way that I can test with a small node script via esbuild bundling. Let me do that — it's valuable to verify the drag math.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the risk areas:\n1. The drag math (positions) — testable via a pure function.\n2. Whether pointer events work in a headless browser — I can't test, but pointer events are standard. One risk: Playwright's `dragTo` uses mouse events (mousedown/mousemove/mouseup) which DO generate pointer events in Chromium (pointerdown/pointermove/pointerup are fired for mouse input). Yes, Chromium fires pointer events for mouse input ✓.\n\nAnother risk: `document.elementFromPoint` returns the element under the pointer; during a drag, if the pointer is over a card, `closest(\"[data-column]\")` finds the column ✓.\n\n3. A potential issue: the `pointerdown` on the card triggers `setDrag` → the effect adds window listeners. But React's state update is async; if the pointer moves immediately, the listener might not be attached yet. Playwright's dragTo does: hover → mousedown → hover target (2 steps) → mouseup. Between mousedown and mouseup there's at least one mouse move, and React will have flushed the state update by then (React 18 batches but flushes before the next event). Since the pointermove handler is attached in a useEffect that runs after the render triggered by setDrag — React processes the state update synchronously-ish before the next browser event? React 18's concurrent rendering schedules the update; the effect runs after the commit, which happens in a microtask/task. Pointer events dispatched by Playwright are separate tasks, so the effect should be attached before the next pointermove ✓.\n\nBut to be extra safe, I could attach the window listeners directly in the pointerdown handler (not via an effect). That removes any dependency on React's render timing. Let me do that: in startDrag, attach the listeners immediately; in pointerup/cancel, remove them.\n\nThat's more robust. Let me refactor useDragAndDrop to attach listeners in startDrag.\n\n4. Another risk: `touch-none` on cards prevents scrolling — fine.\n\n5. The card's `onClick` → openTask → for signed-in users opens the edit dialog. For guests, clicking does nothing ✓ (openTask returns early if !user).\n\n6. The AuthPanel is shown when not signed in ✓.\n\n7. Registration: after registering, the token is stored and the user is signed in ✓.\n\nLet me now refactor the drag hook for robustness and extract the pure math for testing.\n\nPlan for useDragAndDrop:\n\n```ts\nexport interface Rect { top: number; height: number }\n\nexport function computeDropTarget(input: {\n  taskId: number;\n  fromStatus: Status;\n  fromPosition: number;\n  status: Status;\n  others: { id: number; position: number; top: number; height: number }[];\n  pointerY: number;\n}): { index: number; position: number }\n```\n\nThen the hook uses it.\n\nLet me write the pure function in src/lib/drop.ts so it can be unit-tested with node (via esbuild bundling).\n\nThen write a test script test/drop.test.ts run with esbuild+node. Hmm, node can run TS directly? Node 24 supports type stripping for .ts files natively (--experimental-strip-types is on by default in Node 22.6+/23+; in Node 24, TS type stripping is enabled by default). Node 24.22 supports running .ts files directly (erasable syntax only). My drop.ts would use `import type` — erasable ✓. So I can run `node test/drop.test.ts` directly! But the import path must include the .ts extension: `import { computeDropTarget } from \"../src/lib/drop.ts\"` — with type stripping, importing a .ts file works if the specifier includes the extension.\n\nLet me create frontend/tests/drop.test.ts and run it with node.\n\nActually, should I add a test file to the repo? It's fine and shows diligence, but it adds files not requested. I'll keep the test in the repo as `frontend/tests/drop.test.ts` with a \"test\" script in package.json (\"test\": \"node tests/drop.test.ts\"). Hmm, the Dockerfile runs `npm ci` + `npm run build` — extra devDeps are fine.\n\nHmm, but node's type stripping requires the file to have no non-erasable syntax (no enums, no namespaces, no parameter properties) ✓.\n\nLet me do it — it validates the trickiest logic.\n\nLetme now refactor.</think>\n\nLet me refactor the drop-target math into a pure, testable function and make the drag listeners attach immediately:","time":{"start":1794358133370,"end":1794358133393}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794358133417,"sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_01a1c81439bb7714b8b4df70","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"import type { Status } from \"../types\";\n\nexport interface DropCard {\n  id: number;\n  position: number;\n  top: number;\n  height: number;\n}\n\nexport interface DropComputation {\n  /** Insertion index inside the visible cards of the column, the dragged card excluded. */\n  index: number;\n  /** Final 1-based position inside the column. */\n  position: number;\n}\n\n/**\n * Work out where a dragged card lands.\n *\n * `cards` are the visible cards of the target column (the dragged card excluded) with their\n * geometry. The card is inserted before the first card whose middle lies below the pointer,\n * or after the last visible card when the pointer is below all of them.\n */\nexport function computeDropTarget(input: {\n  taskId: number;\n  fromStatus: Status;\n  fromPosition: number;\n  status: Status;\n  cards: DropCard[];\n  pointerY: number;\n}): DropComputation {\n  const { fromStatus, fromPosition, status, cards, pointerY } = input;\n\n  let index = cards.length;\n  let anchor: DropCard | null = null;\n  for (let i = 0; i < cards.length; i += 1) {\n    const card = cards[i];\n    if (pointerY < card.top + card.height / 2) {\n      anchor = card;\n      index = i;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const adjust = (card: DropCard) =>\n    status === fromStatus && card.position > fromPosition ? card.position - 1 : card.position;\n\n  let position: number;\n  if (anchor) {\n    position = adjust(anchor);\n  } else if (cards.length > 0) {\n    position = adjust(cards[cards.length - 1]) + 1;\n  } else {\n    position = 1;\n  }\n\n  return { index, position };\n}\n","filePath":"/workspace/frontend/src/lib/drop.ts"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/workspace/frontend/src/lib/drop.ts","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"frontend/src/lib/drop.ts","time":{"start":1794358133378,"end":1794358133399}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c814427d001w1kKsnLlKXGXVE","sessionID":"ses_e37f28d7bffekBPOL1DKCH4OOV","messageID":"msg_1c8140c15001t5g76apIBFd2op"}}
